::: {.doc-subject}

Delete an edge from the graph by its ID.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L3185-L3308){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.delete_edge_by_id(edge_id)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Delete edge by ID](../assets/graphs/delete-edge-by-id.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Before: a four-node cycle whose edges are labeled with their edge IDs 1 to 5; A and B are joined by two parallel edges, 1 and 5, and edge 5 is highlighted in the accent color. After: only edge 5 is gone, shown dashed, while its parallel twin, edge 1, remains." width="100%"}

Removes an edge directly using its unique edge identifier. This is more efficient than
deleting by source and target nodes when the edge ID is known, and is particularly useful
for multigraphs where multiple edges may exist between the same pair of nodes.

Only the edge is removed; its endpoint nodes stay in the graph. Edge IDs are assigned
automatically when edges are created and can be found with `get_edge_ids()`,
`find_edge_ids()` (all edges between two nodes), selection helpers such as
`get_selected_edge_ids()`, or the `id` column of `get_edge_df()`. The graph is modified in
place and the deletion is recorded in the step log (see `steps`). It behaves the same as
`delete_edge(id=...)`.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[edge_id]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The unique integer identifier of the edge to remove. Must refer to an edge that
    currently exists; IDs of previously deleted edges are not reused.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Returns self to enable method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [EdgeNotFoundError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If no edge exists with the specified ID. It is a subclass of both `KeyError` and
    `ValueError`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~find_edge_ids`) 

:   The IDs of the edges between two nodes.

[](`~delete_edge`) 

:   Remove an edge by its endpoints.

[](`~remove_duplicate_edges`) 

:   Remove all parallel edges at once.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

`delete_edge_by_id()` removes a single edge directly, without resolving source and
target nodes first:

::: {#d8f196e6 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B")])

edge_id = graph.get_edge_ids()[0]
graph.delete_edge_by_id(edge_id=edge_id)

graph.has_edge(source="A", target="B")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
False
```
:::
:::


An ID that doesn't belong to any edge raises an `EdgeNotFoundError`:

::: {#bee99337 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
try:
    graph.delete_edge_by_id(edge_id=999)
except ec.EdgeNotFoundError as e:
    print(e)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Edge with ID 999 does not exist
```
:::
:::


### A double charge

Deleting by ID is the right tool when several edges connect the same pair and only a
specific one should go. Dee was charged twice for the same coffee on 4 March, and paid for
another on 9 March:

::: {#7484a9bc .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
card = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [
        ("Dee", "Grocer", 64, {"date": "2026-03-03"}),
        ("Dee", "Cafe", 12, {"date": "2026-03-04"}),
        ("Dee", "Cafe", 12, {"date": "2026-03-04"}),
        ("Dee", "Cafe", 12, {"date": "2026-03-09"}),
    ],
    directed=True,
)

charges = card.find_edge_ids("Dee", "Cafe")
card.get_edge_df().filter(card.get_edge_df()["id"].is_in(charges))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
